b63c42998c14.png) have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/874fc16afdc829b59ebb3f871e48722f6a11e475577e3195549d68cb2c836d30.png)\n\nAdditionally,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/033debb7dd8e9b9dbd5caa4fbe21557f26979dc19aa16ebd607fa29de34e9f5d.png)\n\nConsequently,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/1b1692325288b047f70aa0aa09921e576c3e61f22ee06b295bc72776a615b223.png)\n\nSince ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/cdd03b199d81550020678e938a618057ec249985289ab595de8701117ff08e67.png) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/af7376fd2f2674cbb36c9a0128d1f90bbfc15082cb11fde35ce8b20a28b9043f.png) we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/f9b2860b8a585a97d625d1a0cad6d1373766af5260d731a173850f5d5c4bbc3c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096374 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "dccb5bac-e528-5b42-9949-36b3870fdde1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4287" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/bfa592bb1f4e5a292e1e94476ab6e5f1.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/49fbbe1f5dd4731054af33666b88cb64.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2 \\mid \\mathbf{n} \\mid$;\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}\n$$\n\n.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2 \\mid \\mathbf{k} \\mid$\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n.\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= ON^(→) + NM^(→) \\\\\n&= 2n + 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k}$, the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096375 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "09d593cc-526e-5691-9a18-efff515eae59" :question/math-academy-id "e-4288" :question/problem "Consider the following diagram, where $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b}$, and $X$ divides $AB$ in the ratio $3:1$. Find $\\overrightarrow{OX}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/8512f8ab1660315b7e5f0aa8beb302ff.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}\n$$\n\nWe're told that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nso we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}$.\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= AO^(→) + OB^(→) \\\\\n&=-OA^(→) + OB^(→) \\\\\n&=-a + b \\\\\n&= b - a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nOX^(→) &= OA^(→) + AX^(→) \\\\\n&= a + \\frac{3}{4}(b - a) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}a + \\frac{3}{4}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "839ed5f5-165f-5de5-aba1-551ac605475f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5212" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/c2827bf97f6d17572e9ac2b808d16a69.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/0b673cb49bd6ea0955e57952763b7458.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup OAC$, we have:\n\n- $AC=2 \\mid \\mathbf{b} \\mid$\n- $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AC}=2\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc &= OA^(→) + AC^(→) \\\\\n&= a + 2b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b}$, the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{b}$, we get:\n$\\lambda=2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244904 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "135258ad-b736-5483-9d0e-8316244c5f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244906} {:answer/id #uuid "f5421aa7-f9fe-52a1-b110-4ab8755d5f87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244907} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ace964-c0d3-5733-8afc-2651f0f6b995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244908} {:answer/id #uuid "f166b6cb-ff5b-578f-a80e-8e3a0babc366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244909} {:answer/id #uuid "1a6eecee-dbd8-5b0b-9365-3dde4970848a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7ace964-c0d3-5733-8afc-2651f0f6b995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244908} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8a1607d-8e13-5c40-820a-e1ffeea20d2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd182d35-c395-5986-8962-70df4f717f72" :question/math-academy-id "q-120245" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/58901401bfe81e040e22da6e07d543423db58a845009a80525eae131752874d4.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+3b$, find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/3effb654f4db5e1f207c671116baf648cb70f90781882a1fd758f32b610aefca.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=2a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=2a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc7095eb-6985-5802-af0c-7428e098f774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244913} {:answer/id #uuid "5022f32c-324b-5430-acb8-1b1c5bfc7fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244914} {:answer/id #uuid "817bc788-aa3f-5cce-a585-dd99a0106a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244915} {:answer/id #uuid "f7699e4a-c740-55bf-968c-bde2e1328b2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244916} {:answer/id #uuid "24d6b208-43b8-5d3e-88a3-189b1a4b59f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24d6b208-43b8-5d3e-88a3-189b1a4b59f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244917} :answer-field/id #uuid "989889d2-e847-571d-b93a-933db73337d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a11bc4a2-fd7a-5333-a516-b057f55e0ba3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120254" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/6882323d2c8017e95100a0ab9d1091fdf149e884ef27d2d2bf59ab7609b1a960.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/a8309dc45ac43c6642010e744de5a062d6575d48ba85bd642df39dd261f47422.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b-2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=-2a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc2ca1ac-baf7-50c5-9c60-57a1ed1533c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244920} {:answer/id #uuid "d4141f81-6004-5a81-ae43-db9641fafd45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244921} {:answer/id #uuid "55438839-19ba-5f55-be85-a1bbb8ca93ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244922} {:answer/id #uuid "18f316b1-e2ff-53ed-a4d7-e3aa849442f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244923} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc728fe-2da8-5566-a283-1e201b0de756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc2ca1ac-baf7-50c5-9c60-57a1ed1533c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244920} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaab8ddf-1251-539a-9910-4a883dc533e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4d3394e1-e3ca-5fef-81fe-c269ab4905cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-120257" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/551316d1e36175ca302c5c472d4109a9bc9cadf32cc85d2a1751424a386070cc.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a-2b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/40a0137d6227212db9b7ef9f349029838f3f51398790ec5e1c304e12c3743a0f.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2b-\\frac{1}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-2b=-\\frac{1}{2}a-2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce5854b7-7cbf-54a7-bc43-e0aa8f2cc5a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{3}{7}a+\\frac{1}{7}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244927} {:answer/id #uuid "175a712f-79d0-59bd-a541-80bd960f4d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{6}{7}a+\\frac{1}{7}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244928} {:answer/id #uuid "e29f2ace-a2fb-50ad-9c19-e8ea22d50da1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{5}{7}a+\\frac{1}{7}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244929} {:answer/id #uuid "c5fe106f-1f79-57fd-99ba-afa80175a687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{6}{7}a-\\frac{1}{7}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244930} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae2ae5c-e84b-5da1-8c8f-d00c187ffe7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=-\\frac{6}{7}a+\\frac{1}{7}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "175a712f-79d0-59bd-a541-80bd960f4d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{6}{7}a+\\frac{1}{7}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244928} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0560624-2f58-52b9-b266-3523a08fa423" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe9c9bfe-4a90-5821-aa8d-37fc516d145c" :question/math-academy-id "q-12214" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/4205d878409776be6c18735910a2f8ece019418452df83cb75d197531f057532.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b$ and $T$ divides $\\overset{―}{AB}$ in the ratio $AT:TB=1:6.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OT}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AT:TB=1:6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AT}=\\frac{1}{7}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{1}{7}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{1}{7}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}a+\\frac{1}{7}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0251127a-7ee5-5a6d-b6b0-b1c20e899942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OZ}=\\frac{2}{7}a-\\frac{5}{7}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244934} {:answer/id #uuid "24758abb-8e23-5ffe-b52f-a7b68823a3fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OZ}=\\frac{1}{7}a-\\frac{6}{7}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244935} {:answer/id #uuid "5c8bd66a-b36e-596f-b076-c272477c69c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OZ}=\\frac{2}{7}a+\\frac{5}{7}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244936} {:answer/id #uuid "faeca566-2f36-5590-9219-6ddc52cd6229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OZ}=\\frac{1}{7}a+\\frac{6}{7}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244937} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e37258-8dc9-58a1-8b66-03755b295a0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OZ}=\\frac{2}{7}a+\\frac{6}{7}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c8bd66a-b36e-596f-b076-c272477c69c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OZ}=\\frac{2}{7}a+\\frac{5}{7}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244936} :answer-field/id #uuid "58e5eb14-3d14-576f-b79c-1bb39c5d049f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e7ffc2f-2233-5a28-a212-753efb96ce30" :question/math-academy-id "q-12233" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/2f0d9df3316615646dfbd994a378293dacf937050514080b5fe66af9794ae9e3.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OY}=b$ and $Z$ divides $\\overset{―}{XY}$ in the ratio $XZ:ZY=5:2.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OZ}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OZ}=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XZ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{XZ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OXY,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{XY} & =\\overset{\\to }{XO}+\\overset{\\to }{OY} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{OY} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $XZ:ZY=5:2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{XZ}=\\frac{5}{7}\\overset{\\to }{XY}=\\frac{5}{7}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OZ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XZ} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{5}{7}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}a+\\frac{5}{7}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "242f36a0-6615-5643-8da3-cc752cf9e8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\frac{1}{3}a+\\frac{2}{3}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244941} {:answer/id #uuid "90790f6c-f1f0-50c2-b1a5-e5a68840c7e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\frac{1}{6}a-\\frac{5}{6}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244942} {:answer/id #uuid "30073e78-f5c6-55fe-b5f7-1960a687caaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\frac{1}{3}a-\\frac{2}{3}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244943} {:answer/id #uuid "2003e33a-9aa2-52c0-a377-aa7258488ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\frac{5}{6}a+\\frac{1}{6}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244944} {:answer/id #uuid "450d2566-2fe6-55cb-9341-a385f27e5555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\frac{1}{6}a+\\frac{5}{6}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "450d2566-2fe6-55cb-9341-a385f27e5555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\frac{1}{6}a+\\frac{5}{6}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244945} :answer-field/id #uuid "64371eeb-c85c-576f-a6c3-c9bc5b62c3e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51a5b8b3-782e-5b64-8b37-655fae98f17f" :question/math-academy-id "q-12235" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/45110269c8a1ca9ad80cabc2f6c44592dca781afde7347d9d0a7e3363a76563a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b$ and $C$ divides $\\overset{―}{AB}$ in the ratio $AC:CB=5:1.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OC}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AC:CB=5:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\frac{5}{6}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{5}{6}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OC} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{5}{6}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}a+\\frac{5}{6}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80120cea-42f6-536a-a12c-4c3b760859bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 1.5$ and $μ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094899} {:answer/id #uuid "90ed0152-bc17-5c0a-9fac-769c5a688387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 2$ and $μ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094900} {:answer/id #uuid "b64b31a2-ea40-5bfb-917e-faf58d123194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2$ and $μ =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094901} {:answer/id #uuid "fe5f92d9-4714-55d4-8102-d106e968b4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2$ and $μ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094902} {:answer/id #uuid "339e78f1-3955-5364-a7de-0d57fddd6b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 2.5$ and $μ =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90ed0152-bc17-5c0a-9fac-769c5a688387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 2$ and $μ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094900} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9f338b7-0e6c-5006-af37-19877673bf78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b7aee39-22ab-5444-970d-797a67e85565" :question/math-academy-id "q-19370" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/q-19370.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c = λa + μb$, find $λ$ and $μ$." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/601e049067de307ef2321ae9b516d1c7306d837ca0758c080074446a8c54389d.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=2a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =2a+2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=2a+2b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2db866fd-2588-5c42-982c-67d6a3b9a76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244948} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba257bf-af61-5cb7-8b7e-d9463d5e5dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2db866fd-2588-5c42-982c-67d6a3b9a76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244948} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4756c5b-1750-559c-a816-407094d8be46" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3fb92e3-522c-5f79-a155-8a768412d58e" :question/math-academy-id "q-321299" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/6fe3721ff3ab63e75be934c79b03d4ca601513c2e08b987a0bafd94c74856e90.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=a+\\lambda b$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/9c8d67718152bc1bc9569213369580b6fc6f5bc1fe0c63b637f14c9f659dfe27.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=\\frac{2}{3}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\frac{2}{3}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{2}{3}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+\\frac{2}{3}b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bfd1f22-b82c-5d60-8942-8eee6131112e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244952} {:answer/id #uuid "a77263ed-2e1b-5871-b785-5b889b9b4ec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bfd1f22-b82c-5d60-8942-8eee6131112e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244952} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c13c6d7-7001-5808-b413-3e3a6b9553aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244951} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de8e71e1-33e8-5960-8404-3b41af8a0fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de8e71e1-33e8-5960-8404-3b41af8a0fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244955} :answer-field/id #uuid "84e587f0-cf75-5361-9026-8c0f1f48cac4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a4338d8-16c5-555f-9994-d62335cb5f49" :question/math-academy-id "q-322576" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/36742531a5f9f5d77c5f7e90980120479f4142cecaf6e84937d76d938688409d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=d,$ $\\overset{\\to }{ON}=e,$ and $T$ divides $\\overset{―}{MN}$ in the ratio $MT:TN=8:3.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OT}$ via the vectors $d$ and $e.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OT}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$d+$\n{{field-2}}\n$e$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=d,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{MT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OMN,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MN} & =\\overset{\\to }{MO}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-d+e \\\\  & =e-d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $MT:TN=8:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{MT}=\\frac{8}{11}\\overset{\\to }{MN}=\\frac{8}{11}(e-d).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MT} \\\\  & =d+\\frac{8}{11}(e-d) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{11}d+\\frac{8}{11}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "470ce3cd-42bc-5c4b-b437-577dcdff76ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "470ce3cd-42bc-5c4b-b437-577dcdff76ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244958} :answer-field/id #uuid "40f767c5-7e54-5938-9d82-7c8b10472571" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244957} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "448a2f1f-50e8-5bca-8efe-153e3defc945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "448a2f1f-50e8-5bca-8efe-153e3defc945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244960} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b36c722-da71-5e3c-9d5d-e002b8c67d3d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "241dbed7-7afd-5efd-afb4-c20895ec0441" :question/math-academy-id "q-326014" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/de5905301a41f02237a48257a79e457f11ad74186942ca5bcf0002ab041aff91.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $t=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/1be50e101b386f53ea5a2f1474f2e1d2debb8f98380d49a346dda32b37db70f1.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=2|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $j$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-2j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=\\frac{1}{2}|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $k$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-\\frac{1}{2}k.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\nt=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2j-\\frac{1}{2}k.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-2j-\\frac{1}{2}k.$\n\nSince $k∦j,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{1}{2}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbe3afe4-b35f-56ef-a685-aca14ac87fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244963} {:answer/id #uuid "bf35e923-932a-5c9e-ae43-917031fc2e04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbe3afe4-b35f-56ef-a685-aca14ac87fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244963} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e94e67e-5d7b-5839-90e0-4522158d9bfa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244962} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c03fd0d1-65d5-5bb1-8e56-820484ec9197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c03fd0d1-65d5-5bb1-8e56-820484ec9197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244966} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9f6968d-16ac-5fcb-a4df-05939de74617" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a354717-6082-50a5-b00c-725aaaa80e78" :question/math-academy-id "q-326018" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/0c6dc7566d3ef4b0c047f048738aee6a204e0ba0e40cc48e83867456c6a1d67f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/563522ad9e5cf8e134a6669364986de592c7d63da244b050ef466e9f6c971a39.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=3|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=3p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =3p+2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=3p+2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dabf9419-e17d-5005-af0a-8e26947fc332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244969} {:answer/id #uuid "c64d41d8-d7b7-56a8-8e73-dc410d1e96de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dabf9419-e17d-5005-af0a-8e26947fc332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244969} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd23321d-1fe6-5fda-a2f3-8a0154800e34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "975e02f7-1c73-5ea9-9bad-deffeacf30d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-327046" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/3d34b42eb1874894af9675944e97e403d67db92bf96449fd8f87fbb14063a10e.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=a+\\lambda b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/c074e514a4ca52868a5c01d7a29182d62493f2cc955fd0582e52c78bdde2bf7d.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f98f4480-07ec-5b85-b225-41142f1ac636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244973} {:answer/id #uuid "f96f8ec3-e71e-5a21-b32a-9bc87cb65f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f96f8ec3-e71e-5a21-b32a-9bc87cb65f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244974} :answer-field/id #uuid "01dc1a2d-47f1-56ae-aa52-c4a1cbbf5ea8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244972} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6b644b5-8659-5893-9dbc-fa8fd40213dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6b644b5-8659-5893-9dbc-fa8fd40213dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244976} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe422f1a-a05f-56be-a9b7-8220326b20e4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "963ee32e-9695-5de4-abd4-883ca842b97a" :question/math-academy-id "q-327075" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/e69402e97668c50f91258ca7e8dbb5206d1cd681e73bb9333303777adc866b70.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $i=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/cb8209592ec6d5be435622999c1b3eb86e1756cbe9852be0efdbbf41d41777ff.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have  \n\n- $OJ=2|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ and $j$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-2j.$  \n\nSimilarly:  \n\n- $JL=|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ and $k$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=k.$  \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\ni=\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2j+k.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $i=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-2j+k.$  \n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way.  Equating the coefficients, we get \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,\\mu =1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b618ba29-f2ef-5ebd-a698-937a01af1adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244979} {:answer/id #uuid "f8112905-742b-5117-9113-54b51ab9f462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244980} {:answer/id #uuid "e8422aa6-e138-5e37-8559-c6815c49b0ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244981} {:answer/id #uuid "5da4f64e-9b77-5a80-b4d0-555c64927105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244982} {:answer/id #uuid "bc05fbf6-7334-5828-aeaf-9f8f78233303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8422aa6-e138-5e37-8559-c6815c49b0ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244981} :answer-field/id #uuid "406a4f26-b5a6-54fa-a816-81d0fa9bf5ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89f49045-ce3b-55cb-aabe-852c9bd4db2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-367052" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/1d424b5db0534a8d9abc9b0aa84dcb2e6860a7ed51de7390fc4f7e91c1c33f2b.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+4b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+4b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $4b.$ So, we first draw the vector $4b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/d8fdfb800bd9bbb179d51115b86823d99029732fee9810281b9c587a32376993.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $4b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=4|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-4a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =4b-4a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+4b=-4a+4b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5adb761a-5ee3-5a9c-a481-e67dc42cf3e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244986} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8e928b-008f-5097-a34a-af987c244edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244987} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa7dfcc-ed31-5de3-a478-7eaac032dec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244988} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9cb417-86a7-5086-b6b2-b961ec6125b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244989} {:answer/id #uuid "c68a70a4-592d-5a77-8c21-90def35faa54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c68a70a4-592d-5a77-8c21-90def35faa54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244990} :answer-field/id #uuid "9272d607-3e30-5efb-b82a-ec6ebb013a6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9e9417d0-f9bf-576f-9ea8-f8ad0d1c238b" :question/math-academy-id "q-367060" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/d7757a33bfef4f199fe78262f52bb683521eba62fd6af29c953f8ca9088e46ad.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a-2b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/e219cc0b1277058339c182a0849592a245645272eabde16cd241df6d9206691f.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2b-2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-2b=-2a-2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29336052-c4a9-582d-9220-3ee636684796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=-\\frac{1}{3}v+\\frac{2}{3}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244993} {:answer/id #uuid "3daf1a81-0ba1-59df-8131-cdad3411a73b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{1}{3}u+\\frac{2}{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244994} {:answer/id #uuid "2d2fc91a-3744-579e-a0f6-950e4a878a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244995} {:answer/id #uuid "b54899df-6d8c-5fd5-a623-369f31e61ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{2}{3}u+\\frac{1}{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244996} {:answer/id #uuid "4f35be66-b00c-5e55-b9b7-7a0b8eb76352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}v-\\frac{2}{9}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d2fc91a-3744-579e-a0f6-950e4a878a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244995} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee483764-dc6f-568b-bd11-a98dda2f857a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2af4a406-e134-5401-990f-61dd08996b3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-42669" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/040127594f639248736d4492f1853bcb1e6ef18c37db9242ebe466045713628a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OV}=v$ and $W$ divides $\\overset{―}{UV}$ in the ratio $UW:WV=2:7.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OW}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{UW}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OUV,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =\\overset{\\to }{UO}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-u+v \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $UW:WV=2:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{UW}=\\frac{2}{9}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=\\frac{2}{9}(v-u).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OW} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =u+\\frac{2}{9}(v-u) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99c93002-cec0-585e-b298-bb22a91e4594" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245000} {:answer/id #uuid "5367a754-19df-5f95-9885-3de66cf35990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245001} {:answer/id #uuid "6607f77f-8ed2-5f57-91df-e033dd3f08c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245002} {:answer/id #uuid "ab499aeb-edbe-5caa-905b-f6dcdb15c507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245003} {:answer/id #uuid "b73f99f1-d98a-57cb-b1c5-0d842b265a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99c93002-cec0-585e-b298-bb22a91e4594" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245000} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c384cc2-32be-5ffa-aa18-e0023add5217" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43595d80-5aaf-5e64-b428-80dba6e1b370" :question/math-academy-id "q-70141" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/068eeaf4f0ac63aafca9ee09f06b94f6e52cbe00b4a625970881fe105e299a40.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/45a4735bf7710ff7c2699a9b42b10c34807d6162a3d7da38842ba87f13158788.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=\\frac{1}{2}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\frac{1}{2}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{1}{2}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+\\frac{1}{2}b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6aeda10d-fba0-5820-a7c0-3a0d52f3a952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245007} {:answer/id #uuid "2caaaca2-5a24-5fbb-9e8a-f96b5d044c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245008} {:answer/id #uuid "51e61555-46ef-50dd-b7d5-b4e2b97c2391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245009} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fab4bc-e285-5597-a86b-24901d1c709d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245010} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0d4625-60b6-5012-9cd1-4702b0ea4946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6aeda10d-fba0-5820-a7c0-3a0d52f3a952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245007} :answer-field/id #uuid "4770dbe4-e2d5-5e6d-90fe-d18f644c08b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "40feffa6-3562-5269-909d-f5b683d00558" :question/math-academy-id "q-70148" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/d3f77b108decdfe95fe06d1ace87ee7f844a4f937f8b67a9f14e750a09ae4d57.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a-b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/7f29124776f69f370b3129313fd1c2fa850924f446c6f086dcacd170f1ca6d24.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2.5|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2.5a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-b-2.5a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-b=-2.5a-b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2.5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e38d6425-8868-598a-a9d8-639e31969928" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245014} {:answer/id #uuid "f4378990-c9c5-5f8c-b4a1-0bce2aa38c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245015} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd6ad5a-65f8-556c-b240-b4619ae62dc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245016} {:answer/id #uuid "61cd456c-0ff2-5f51-b8b2-5ee6e389a247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245017} {:answer/id #uuid "7451b8aa-8c00-5672-a403-166440bd3b68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7451b8aa-8c00-5672-a403-166440bd3b68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245018} :answer-field/id #uuid "b952bbba-e88a-5586-83bf-0fe5826ccfd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c73c9ba5-ea87-560b-a6b2-b6be46851371" :question/math-academy-id "q-70187" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/245a7b9af068b057e16a0c84971f5bb356a9fc56374cb3c17c4896d7a9b815ca.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $b.$ So, we begin with the vector $b,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/9b99ad62bfedf6945020d345ef0bbc3531c58af1088698a78daf482cd4c14fe1.png)\n\nNote that in $△OBC,$ we have:\n\n- $BC=4|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=4a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =b+4a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+b=b+4a.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "578432c0-a34d-58b0-80ea-88acb989d650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245021} {:answer/id #uuid "3590535d-fdb1-5314-a9cd-d5c23ca08f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245022} {:answer/id #uuid "fabe72ec-9447-51e3-a477-6d231308242b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245023} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c7d16f-ef6a-5fb2-b94a-7d13756be6ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245024} {:answer/id #uuid "f6de3dd8-871e-54ff-9959-c1ad1f66e357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6de3dd8-871e-54ff-9959-c1ad1f66e357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245025} :answer-field/id #uuid "27945f34-a1a4-5bda-8afb-726a7bef5b7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42df49c5-88ed-5ab5-9dd5-9572a242592b" :question/math-academy-id "q-77515" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/c800644be3cc0ad0688d52948a4a2cb813cbe14b1fe899abe5a1d92613a687bf.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/90272fad8af526de6e169faba8daae29210c1f502c4959d0e1829e5ad65476f0.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=2|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=2p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=4|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=4q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =2p+4q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=2p+4q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =4$"}]}
